[{:db/id "q-113750/selection/74053cd8f212994373f56db80fbf0eade2779b20bfbeb0bc87c25a146cc918d6" :answer/id #uuid "c5c5fc8d-7493-57d2-b12a-fd3be232c6cd" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$f(x)$ does not have any local maxima" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-113750/selection/4e2cb386499e1926f074a7bcd422a63175a237204a3823f0fa6c86b56d301873" :answer/id #uuid "f772e051-657c-598b-953d-aefbfeca8e9f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-113750/selection/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" :answer/id #uuid "49731ccb-509a-5c99-8306-e8a84b760082" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-113750/selection/681a4b64a7bb464b7f554c4e26477e3547d3c60cd353fce5dda4ae108b1c4fa5" :answer/id #uuid "95862677-e4f3-545a-8efd-e2a2122f2379" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-113750/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "631dcd5d-8feb-5ea0-879b-8b738b9f80d7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-113750/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "6b571ce7-3c29-5354-85c0-f9325e4fd427" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-113750/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer-field/choices ["q-113750/selection/74053cd8f212994373f56db80fbf0eade2779b20bfbeb0bc87c25a146cc918d6" "q-113750/selection/4e2cb386499e1926f074a7bcd422a63175a237204a3823f0fa6c86b56d301873" "q-113750/selection/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" "q-113750/selection/681a4b64a7bb464b7f554c4e26477e3547d3c60cd353fce5dda4ae108b1c4fa5" "q-113750/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-113750" :question/id #uuid "b49a83d0-be9c-5ea2-af93-457dbf50bb25" :question/math-academy-id "q-113750" :question/problem "For which value of $x$ does the function $f(x)=12x+9{x}^{2}-4{x}^{3}$ have a local maximum?" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =12+18x-12{x}^{2} \\\\  & =-6(2{x}^{2}-3x-2) \\\\  & =-6(x-2)(2x+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n-6(x-2)(2x+1)=0\\,⟹\\,x=-\\frac{1}{2},2.\n$$\n\nSo we have two stationary points, $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $x=2.$ Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-1,0,3$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-1) & =-6(-1-2)(2(-1)+1)=-18<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =-6(0-2)(2(0)+1)=12>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(3) & =-6(3-2)(2(3)+1)=-42<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-\\frac{1}{2})$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $(-\\frac{1}{2},2)$ | $2$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-1$ |  | $0$ |  | $3$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↘$ | min | $↗$ | max | $↘$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical points.\n\n- At $x=-\\frac{1}{2},$ the first derivative changes sign from negative to positive. Therefore, $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ is a local minimum.\n\n- At $x=2,$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=2$ is a local maximum.\n\nWe conclude that $f(x)$ has one local maximum at $x=2.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-113750/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "33965139-0c6a-50fc-993a-e2acd6760212"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-113750"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-344287/field-1/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer/id #uuid "3369ea3e-3a20-591e-8b28-8e30cae2ee49" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-344287/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "dc5877b8-a4d7-5cb9-a4d6-9000754c3791" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-344287/field-1/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer-field/choices ["q-344287/field-1/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-344287" :question/id #uuid "889709cf-f226-587b-b510-b013e95ddfc7" :question/math-academy-id "q-344287" :question/problem "For which value of $x$ does the function $f(x)=2{x}^{3}-15{x}^{2}+24x+1$ have a local minimum? Do not round the answer.\n\nThe local minimum is at $x=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=6{x}^{2}-30x+24.\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n6{x}^{2}-30x+24=0\\,⟹\\,x=1,\\,4.\n$$\n\nSo we have two stationary points, $x=1$ and $x=4.$ Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=0,2,5$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(0) & =6(0)^{2}-30(0)+24=24>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =6(2)^{2}-30(2)+24=-12<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(5) & =6(5)^{2}-30(5)+24=24>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,1)$ | $1$ | $(1,4)$ | $4$ | $(4,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $0$ |  | $2$ |  | $5$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | max | $↘$ | min | $↗$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical points.\n\n- At $x=1,$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=1$ is a local maximum.\n\n- At $x=4,$ the first derivative changes sign from negative to positive. Therefore, $x=4$ is a local minimum.\n\nWe conclude that $f(x)$ has one local minimum at $x=4.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-344287/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "33965139-0c6a-50fc-993a-e2acd6760212"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-344287"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-113758/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "d34bc2f3-d2f5-5136-900d-dee8622b2d33" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-113758/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "c5de79bd-d4ad-5727-83f3-fb1c45b3528b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-113758/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "8be996ef-fde7-5ee9-b061-835bac1ce055" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-113758/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "3755b882-caad-5740-a693-c2910f9cd73a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-113758/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "ab6412de-3305-5998-962d-82a19fb91481" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-113758/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "1149029e-4122-51cd-a2aa-0980a00ac29d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-113758/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer-field/choices ["q-113758/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-113758/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-113758/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-113758/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-113758/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-113758" :question/id #uuid "2f661c07-7884-5002-810c-d6680fa001b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-113758" :question/problem "For the function $f(x)=2{x}^{3}+5{x}^{2}+1,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $x=-\\frac{5}{3}$ is a local maximum\n- $x=0$ is a local minimum\n- $x=\\frac{5}{3}$ is a local minimum" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =6{x}^{2}+10x \\\\  & =2x(3x+5).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x(3x+5)\\,⟹\\,x=0,-\\frac{5}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have two stationary points, $x=0$ and $x=-\\frac{5}{3}.$ Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-2,-1,1$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-2) & =2(-2)(3(-2)+5)=4>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(-1) & =2(-1)(3(-1)+5)=-4<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =2(1)(3(1)+5)=16>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-\\frac{5}{3})$ | $-\\frac{5}{3}$ | $(-\\frac{5}{3},0)$ | $0$ | $(0,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-2$ |  | $-1$ |  | $1$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | max | $↘$ | min | $↗$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical points.\n\n- At $x=-\\frac{5}{3},$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=-\\frac{5}{3}$ is a local maximum.\n\n- At $x=0,$ the first derivative changes sign from negative to positive. Therefore, $x=0$ is a local minimum.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-113758/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "33965139-0c6a-50fc-993a-e2acd6760212"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-113758"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-344262/field-1/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "cd68adc4-d1bb-5ad7-b8a2-251cbb7c4158" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-344262/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "15a699aa-48fa-5e54-b0d7-946780762be2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-344262/field-1/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer-field/choices ["q-344262/field-1/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-344262" :question/id #uuid "62d1473d-f510-5d86-b244-10afe12d98ff" :question/math-academy-id "q-344262" :question/problem "For which value of $x$ does the function $f(x)=-{x}^{3}+27x+4$ have a local maximum? Do not round the answer.\n\nThe local maximum is at $x=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=-3{x}^{2}+27.\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n-3{x}^{2}+27=0\\,⟹\\,x=\\pm 3.\n$$\n\nSo we have two stationary points, $x=\\pm 3.$ Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-4,0,4$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-4) & =-3(-4)^{2}+27=-21<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =-3(0)^{2}+27=27>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(4) & =-3(4)^{2}+27=-21<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-3)$ | $-3$ | $(-3,3)$ | $3$ | $(3,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-4$ |  | $0$ |  | $4$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↘$ | min | $↗$ | max | $↘$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical points.\n\n- At $x=-3,$ the first derivative changes sign from negative to positive. Therefore, $x=-3$ is a local minimum.\n\n- At $x=3,$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=3$ is a local maximum.\n\nWe conclude that $f(x)$ has one local maximum at $x=3.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-344262/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "33965139-0c6a-50fc-993a-e2acd6760212"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-344262"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-314703/field-1/df4ef3a75f6d71027299791921d9536d50890079e16123ff435f8ed8ac6150b0" :answer/id #uuid "846ad0e7-df74-5099-a893-9baa0f717945" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\sqrt{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-314703/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "46bf78eb-50a7-54fe-8c91-dbab6674c68f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-314703/field-1/df4ef3a75f6d71027299791921d9536d50890079e16123ff435f8ed8ac6150b0" :answer-field/choices ["q-314703/field-1/df4ef3a75f6d71027299791921d9536d50890079e16123ff435f8ed8ac6150b0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-314703" :question/id #uuid "67afb2de-3f65-5cb2-aa6a-9b3e99320342" :question/math-academy-id "q-314703" :question/problem "For which value of $x$ does the function $f(x)={x}^{3}-6x+4$ have a local maximum? Do not round the answer.\n\nThe local maximum is at $x=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=3{x}^{2}-6.\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n3{x}^{2}-6=0\\,⟹\\,x=\\pm \\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nSo we have two stationary points, $x=\\pm \\sqrt{2}.$ Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-2,0,2$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-2) & =3(-2)^{2}-6=6>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =3(0)^{2}-6=-6<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =3(2)^{2}-6=6>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-\\sqrt{2})$ | $-\\sqrt{2}$ | $(-\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2})$ | $\\sqrt{2}$ | $(\\sqrt{2},\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-2$ |  | $0$ |  | $2$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | max | $↘$ | min | $↗$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical points.\n\n- At $x=-\\sqrt{2},$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=-\\sqrt{2}$ is a local maximum.\n\n- At $x=\\sqrt{2},$ the first derivative changes sign from negative to positive. Therefore, $x=\\sqrt{2}$ is a local minimum.\n\nWe conclude that $f(x)$ has one local maximum at $x=-\\sqrt{2}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-314703/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "33965139-0c6a-50fc-993a-e2acd6760212"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-314703"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-22501/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "7408907c-e638-5728-892d-d19804c476bc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-22501/selection/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" :answer/id #uuid "35c1bc12-8a23-5e99-a59c-b51954ef49ba" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-22501/selection/a2301b8122c312044fb5dc0b0c60c599b4f2f7f4ffcc198c9893873ab9165c9d" :answer/id #uuid "b5364446-c159-5f41-9d99-678e6b083e77" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "e" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-22501/selection/74053cd8f212994373f56db80fbf0eade2779b20bfbeb0bc87c25a146cc918d6" :answer/id #uuid "cda5cfa3-6b2b-5b8e-9980-aa6978d6050f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$f(x)$ does not have any local maxima" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-22501/selection/be7671dfcec88fd83021721c027e180f94aa5dc830cb53553598c14eaf369bc4" :answer/id #uuid "414c2c7a-01d6-542a-b4ec-3021f054d02f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{e}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-22501/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "b80ebf01-631d-59e2-a551-f7d4cbd39e75" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-22501/selection/74053cd8f212994373f56db80fbf0eade2779b20bfbeb0bc87c25a146cc918d6" :answer-field/choices ["q-22501/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" "q-22501/selection/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" "q-22501/selection/a2301b8122c312044fb5dc0b0c60c599b4f2f7f4ffcc198c9893873ab9165c9d" "q-22501/selection/74053cd8f212994373f56db80fbf0eade2779b20bfbeb0bc87c25a146cc918d6" "q-22501/selection/be7671dfcec88fd83021721c027e180f94aa5dc830cb53553598c14eaf369bc4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-22501" :question/id #uuid "e4a28e9c-530c-53b5-badb-fe10a89204bb" :question/math-academy-id "q-22501" :question/problem "For which value of $x$ does the function $f(x)=x{e}^{x}$ have a local maximum?" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=(1)\\cdot {e}^{x}+x{e}^{x}=(1+x){e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nSolving for ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1+x){e}^{x} & =0 \\\\ 1+x & =0 \\\\ x & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $x=-1$ is a stationary point. Next, to determine whether this point is a local maximum or minimum, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-2,0$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-2) & =(1+(-2)){e}^{-2}=-{e}^{-2}<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =(1+0){e}^{0}=1>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-1)$ | $-1$ | $(-1,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-2$ |  | $0$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↘$ | min | $↗$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical point.\n\n- At $x=-1,$ the first derivative changes sign from negative to positive. Therefore, $x=-1$ is a local minimum.\n\nWe conclude that $f(x)$ does not have any local maxima." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-22501/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dbad38a0-e6cb-563a-8e9c-1cee08397083"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-22501"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-49024/selection/74053cd8f212994373f56db80fbf0eade2779b20bfbeb0bc87c25a146cc918d6" :answer/id #uuid "ccf978c7-66b2-536c-bc0f-c66ec44d098e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$f(x)$ does not have any local maxima" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-49024/selection/a2301b8122c312044fb5dc0b0c60c599b4f2f7f4ffcc198c9893873ab9165c9d" :answer/id #uuid "5a4f79fc-fcd6-53fd-9e57-3eb075462ebc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "e" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-49024/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "c06a2f88-7e52-526e-b7eb-a215c8ae05fb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-49024/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "22d9769c-872c-51f6-af5f-0e8b1fd36f19" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-49024/selection/be7671dfcec88fd83021721c027e180f94aa5dc830cb53553598c14eaf369bc4" :answer/id #uuid "b40b9d3a-81f5-5efd-bfae-5d3f85c57737" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{e}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-49024/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "eef4f50c-dc6a-5c61-aa9b-9f3c102f3f7d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-49024/selection/a2301b8122c312044fb5dc0b0c60c599b4f2f7f4ffcc198c9893873ab9165c9d" :answer-field/choices ["q-49024/selection/74053cd8f212994373f56db80fbf0eade2779b20bfbeb0bc87c25a146cc918d6" "q-49024/selection/a2301b8122c312044fb5dc0b0c60c599b4f2f7f4ffcc198c9893873ab9165c9d" "q-49024/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" "q-49024/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" "q-49024/selection/be7671dfcec88fd83021721c027e180f94aa5dc830cb53553598c14eaf369bc4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-49024" :question/id #uuid "9ceddcae-d555-5f79-8be6-8c64add94b83" :question/math-academy-id "q-49024" :question/problem "For which value of $x$ does the function $f(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡x}{x}$ defined for $x\\in (0,\\infty )$ have a local maximum?" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡x}{x}=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10)}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{x}\\cdot x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10)\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}{{x}^{2}{ln}^{2}⁡(10)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ on $(0,\\infty )$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10)} & =0 \\\\ 1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡x & =1 \\\\ x & =e.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $x=e$ is a stationary point. Next, to determine whether this point is a local maximum or minimum, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=1,{e}^{2}$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)}{(1)^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10)}=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10)}>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}({e}^{2}) & =\\frac{1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{2})}{(e)^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10)}=-\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10)}<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(0,e)$ | $e$ | $(e,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $1$ |  | ${e}^{2}$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | max | $↘$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical point.\n\n- At $x=e,$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=e$ is a local maximum.\n\nWe conclude that $f(x)$ has a local maximum at $x=e.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-49024/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dbad38a0-e6cb-563a-8e9c-1cee08397083"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-49024"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-49022"] :question/problem "For which value of $x$ does the function $f(x)={x}^{2}{e}^{x}$ have a local minimum?" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={x}^{2}\\cdot {e}^{x}+2x{e}^{x}=(x+2)x{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$  gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+2)x{e}^{x} & =0 \\\\ (x+2)x & =0 \\\\ x & =-2,0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $x=-2$ and $x=0$ are stationary points. Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-3,-1,1$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-3) & =(-3+2)(-3){e}^{-3}=3{e}^{-3}>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(-1) & =(-1+2)(-1){e}^{-1}=-{e}^{-1}<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =(1+2)(1){e}^{1}=3e>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-2)$ | $-2$ | $(-2,0)$ | $0$ | $(0,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-3$ |  | $-1$ |  | $1$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | max | $↘$ | min | $↗$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical points.\n\n- At $x=-2,$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=-2$ is a local maximum.\n\n- At $x=0,$ the first derivative changes sign from negative to positive. Therefore, $x=0$ is a local minimum.\n\nWe conclude that $f(x)$ has a local minimum at $x=0.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-21496/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "8d52680e-7eed-5d88-987a-bb657a222a8d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-21496/selection/af5580a233ee3709064249d6732fc822dfb93f38caf90c025deb51b127e90b2c" :answer/id #uuid "f01933e8-fc01-5fd9-9f4d-b3dbe908bae8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-21496/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "ffdb8a5e-89fe-5d1a-a03c-ed9a48d30c89" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-21496/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer/id #uuid "95b90f36-2bfc-537a-bace-68c832c0b58e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-21496/selection/2edf353dc47d221855e5e7ee4be942392390ff01766e089c29dc368c2051b12d" :answer/id #uuid "79cafe49-1701-5061-998f-26f103e67760" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-21496/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "bea87e49-0054-52bb-a25a-c4a0114c85fb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-21496/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer-field/choices ["q-21496/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" "q-21496/selection/af5580a233ee3709064249d6732fc822dfb93f38caf90c025deb51b127e90b2c" "q-21496/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" "q-21496/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" "q-21496/selection/2edf353dc47d221855e5e7ee4be942392390ff01766e089c29dc368c2051b12d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-21496" :question/id #uuid "6bb098c4-0b9d-56fe-8eb4-70ba83a986f8" :question/math-academy-id "q-21496" :question/problem "For which value of $x$ does the function $f(x)={x}^{3}+\\frac{48}{x}$ defined for $x\\in (0,\\infty )$ have a local minimum?" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =3{x}^{2}-\\frac{48}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ on $(0,\\infty )$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-\\frac{48}{{x}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{4}-48 & =0 \\\\ 3({x}^{4}-16) & =0 \\\\ 3({x}^{2}+4)({x}^{2}-4) & =0 \\\\ 3({x}^{2}+4)(x+2)(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =\\pm 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $x=2$ is a stationary point on $(0,\\infty ).$ Next, to determine whether this point is a local maximum or minimum, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=1,3$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(1) & =3(1)^{2}-\\frac{48}{{1}^{2}}=-45<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(3) & =3(3)^{2}-\\frac{48}{{3}^{2}}=27-\\frac{16}{3}=\\frac{65}{3}>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(0,2)$ | $2$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $1$ |  | $3$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↘$ | min | $↗$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical point.\n\n- At $x=2,$ the first derivative changes sign from negative to positive. Therefore, $x=2$ is a local minimum.\n\nWe conclude that $f(x)$ has a local minimum at $x=2.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-21496/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dbad38a0-e6cb-563a-8e9c-1cee08397083"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-21496"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-22810"] :question/problem "For the function $f(x)=x-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ defined for $x\\in (0,2\\pi ),$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ is a local minimum\n- $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ is a local maximum\n- $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{3}$ is a local maximum" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$   on $(0,2\\pi )$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\,\\,\\frac{5\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ and $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{3}$ are stationary points. Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points  $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\pi ,\\frac{11\\pi }{6}$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & =1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=1-\\sqrt{3}<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(\\pi ) & =1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi =3>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(\\frac{11\\pi }{6}) & =1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{11\\pi }{6})=1-\\sqrt{3}<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(0,\\frac{\\pi }{3})$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ | $(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{5\\pi }{3})$ | $\\frac{5\\pi }{3}$ | $(\\frac{5\\pi }{3},2\\pi )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ |  | $\\pi $ |  | $\\frac{11\\pi }{6}$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↘$ | min | $↗$ | max | $↘$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical points.\n\n- At $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3},$ the first derivative changes sign from negative to positive. Therefore, $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ is a local minimum.\n\n- At $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{3},$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{3}$ is a local maximum.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-79001/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "1ce8789c-445e-5549-93a6-70a74a3ce981" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-79001/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "7222a074-187f-59bd-a74d-5cfb174f1fd5" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-79001/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "e5515bc2-998f-5f72-bc4c-2343a4dfee35" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-79001/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "2f8eee3b-af6e-5563-8933-55f19dc50656" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-79001/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "8e5c16c2-895e-521a-919a-e1e87c48f797" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-79001/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "88615d9c-3523-584a-99fc-2013f6fabcd6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-79001/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer-field/choices ["q-79001/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-79001/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-79001/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-79001/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-79001/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-79001" :question/id #uuid "cfa42c7e-dc08-577a-b09e-d8864c39ea66" :question/math-academy-id "q-79001" :question/problem "Given the function $f(x)=2+2{x}^{3}-{x}^{4},$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $x=\\frac{3}{2}$ is a local maximum\n- $x=0$ is neither a local minimum nor a local maximum\n- $x=0$ is a local minimum" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=6{x}^{2}-4{x}^{3}=2{x}^{2}(3-2x).\n$$\n\nAnd now find the stationary points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}(3-2x)=0\\,⟹\\,x=0\\,\\text{or}\\,x=\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have two stationary points, $x=0,\\,\\frac{3}{2}.$ Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-1,1,2$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-1) & =2(-1)^{2}(3-2(-1))=10>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =2(1)^{2}(3-2(1))=2>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =2(2)^{2}(3-2(2))=-8<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,0)$ | $0$ | $(0,\\frac{3}{2})$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $(\\frac{3}{2},\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-1$ |  | $1$ |  | $2$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ |  | $↗$ | max | $↘$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical points.\n\n- At $x=0,$ the first derivative does not change the sign. Therefore, $x=0$ is neither a local minimum nor a local maximum.\n\n- At $x=\\frac{3}{2},$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=\\frac{3}{2}$ is a local maximum.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-79001/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff7c597d-d4a4-5567-add6-eac77475b0d6"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-79001"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-113766/selection/3e861be81d35c8d4cfc14cc67d7919f2650d5e9bc654eb4705de4f11578bdcff" :answer/id #uuid "45a3a061-a800-5112-893e-925cf6f9a290" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "There is a local minimum at $x=0$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-113766/selection/c112c665879534c7ad1d1042a8b28b7e5f3f70117c93b42716ffdd8ce86d808a" :answer/id #uuid "bb453fa6-c93b-5289-9d2b-6761fa2d7ef8" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The function $f(x)$ has no local extrema" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-113766/selection/f7e0800a377a7e10703ce82b063e43b5665422ee38a1af86d72cc36c4ff07857" :answer/id #uuid "afa37fd1-3952-5fc2-b9d3-d0cb6bd7fed9" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "There is a local maximum at $x=0$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-113766/selection/0b6f58ac489b22373faaa459298a48d1cc3fbd9f7a139b9eb412cc52b65a7190" :answer/id #uuid "b5a921ff-2e7e-5f37-8002-44f09707b253" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "There is a local maximum at $x=11$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-113766/selection/2db22c91c5b28b9b7bae08d309a7456166e51211af958ea5a1e5c5f4be947655" :answer/id #uuid "8bd99e40-a89e-585a-990b-edcc37a88070" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "There is a local minimum at $x=11$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-113766/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "511549b4-61fb-542d-bc7c-47eaab55ad49" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-113766/selection/c112c665879534c7ad1d1042a8b28b7e5f3f70117c93b42716ffdd8ce86d808a" :answer-field/choices ["q-113766/selection/3e861be81d35c8d4cfc14cc67d7919f2650d5e9bc654eb4705de4f11578bdcff" "q-113766/selection/c112c665879534c7ad1d1042a8b28b7e5f3f70117c93b42716ffdd8ce86d808a" "q-113766/selection/f7e0800a377a7e10703ce82b063e43b5665422ee38a1af86d72cc36c4ff07857" "q-113766/selection/0b6f58ac489b22373faaa459298a48d1cc3fbd9f7a139b9eb412cc52b65a7190" "q-113766/selection/2db22c91c5b28b9b7bae08d309a7456166e51211af958ea5a1e5c5f4be947655"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-113766" :question/id #uuid "b349904b-6249-5c3c-b3c7-bf9319607046" :question/math-academy-id "q-113766" :question/problem "Given the function $f(x)={x}^{11},$ which of the following statements are true?" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=11{x}^{10}.\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n11{x}^{10}=0\\,⟹\\,x=0.\n$$\n\nSo $x=0$ is the only stationary point of $f(x).$ Next, to determine whether this point is local maximum or minimum, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-1,1$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-1) & =11(-1)^{10}=11>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =11(1)^{10}=11>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,0)$ | $0$ | $(0,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-1$ |  | $1$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ |  | $↗$ |\n\nThe first derivative does not change sign at $x=0.$ Therefore, we conclude that $x=0$ is neither a minimum nor a maximum.\n\nHence, the first derivative test shows that the function has no local extrema." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-113766/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff7c597d-d4a4-5567-add6-eac77475b0d6"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-113766"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-49035/selection/e07e4b4b7bf0a6b155505349a4f8c40ed1d2fa46a54226484b29bff7403d7dfc" :answer/id #uuid "ea4fbafb-8a83-515e-98f4-a4ab95fcc039" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "There is a local minimum at $x=2$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-49035/selection/3e861be81d35c8d4cfc14cc67d7919f2650d5e9bc654eb4705de4f11578bdcff" :answer/id #uuid "4b5ed1c8-16bc-5417-a259-3f27f7688a8a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "There is a local minimum at $x=0$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-49035/selection/c6fe7d08b208daa22ed80928c22ab75155a7856c58db6c80ba1a41ac252e66a9" :answer/id #uuid "0efdd25c-a9e2-55ab-a249-a953db365c0d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "There is a local maximum at $x=2$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-49035/selection/f7e0800a377a7e10703ce82b063e43b5665422ee38a1af86d72cc36c4ff07857" :answer/id #uuid "33f4cd0b-b327-59f9-bfa1-c42341ea4659" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "There is a local maximum at $x=0$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-49035/selection/c112c665879534c7ad1d1042a8b28b7e5f3f70117c93b42716ffdd8ce86d808a" :answer/id #uuid "036384c0-3ff8-5c09-9941-904335813c3b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The function $f(x)$ has no local extrema" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-49035/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "1ec30606-b998-52be-b98d-f8b320073278" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-49035/selection/c112c665879534c7ad1d1042a8b28b7e5f3f70117c93b42716ffdd8ce86d808a" :answer-field/choices ["q-49035/selection/e07e4b4b7bf0a6b155505349a4f8c40ed1d2fa46a54226484b29bff7403d7dfc" "q-49035/selection/3e861be81d35c8d4cfc14cc67d7919f2650d5e9bc654eb4705de4f11578bdcff" "q-49035/selection/c6fe7d08b208daa22ed80928c22ab75155a7856c58db6c80ba1a41ac252e66a9" "q-49035/selection/f7e0800a377a7e10703ce82b063e43b5665422ee38a1af86d72cc36c4ff07857" "q-49035/selection/c112c665879534c7ad1d1042a8b28b7e5f3f70117c93b42716ffdd8ce86d808a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-49035" :question/id #uuid "a38627ef-e4b2-52dc-9751-9805f2cf3368" :question/math-academy-id "q-49035" :question/problem "Given the function $f(x)={x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}+12x-5,$ which of the following statements are true?" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =3{x}^{2}-12x+12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-12x+12 & =0 \\\\ 3({x}^{2}-4x+4) & =0 \\\\ 3(x-2)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $x=2$ is the only stationary point of $f(x).$ Next, to determine whether this point is a local maximum or minimum, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=0,3$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(0) & =3(0)^{2}-12(0)+12=12>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(3) & =3(3)^{2}-12(3)+12=3>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,2)$ | $2$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $0$ |  | $3$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ |  | $↗$ |\n\nThe first derivative does not change sign at $x=2.$ Therefore, we conclude that $x=2$ is neither a minimum nor a maximum.\n\nHence, the first derivative test shows that the function has no local extrema." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-49035/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff7c597d-d4a4-5567-add6-eac77475b0d6"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-49035"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-49032/selection/3e861be81d35c8d4cfc14cc67d7919f2650d5e9bc654eb4705de4f11578bdcff" :answer/id #uuid "dcf0b828-522c-5854-af67-20e99c73f09e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "There is a local minimum at $x=0$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-49032/selection/d9b70cb012b8a98348e0eb2b076c0ffaa03d2f9b5ed0b778c333cec60818df33" :answer/id #uuid "a54917cb-f4a0-5130-b8b3-829a0cc0328d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "There is a local maximum at $x=-1$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-49032/selection/f7e0800a377a7e10703ce82b063e43b5665422ee38a1af86d72cc36c4ff07857" :answer/id #uuid "0a9796bd-8c80-5229-8ee9-2e4f9f0fc277" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "There is a local maximum at $x=0$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-49032/selection/c112c665879534c7ad1d1042a8b28b7e5f3f70117c93b42716ffdd8ce86d808a" :answer/id #uuid "71e66523-89ce-50ce-a0e9-99c78eb8541a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The function $f(x)$ has no local extrema" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-49032/selection/acc83c74f03748b2e35d16f5b1d34df696d35c1cc9214487ece6a3d7d0147310" :answer/id #uuid "935d0875-03e7-556a-b30f-f199defdd249" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "There is a local minimum at $x=1$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-49032/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "81ce1b99-1050-507a-8734-0b893720346f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-49032/selection/c112c665879534c7ad1d1042a8b28b7e5f3f70117c93b42716ffdd8ce86d808a" :answer-field/choices ["q-49032/selection/3e861be81d35c8d4cfc14cc67d7919f2650d5e9bc654eb4705de4f11578bdcff" "q-49032/selection/d9b70cb012b8a98348e0eb2b076c0ffaa03d2f9b5ed0b778c333cec60818df33" "q-49032/selection/f7e0800a377a7e10703ce82b063e43b5665422ee38a1af86d72cc36c4ff07857" "q-49032/selection/c112c665879534c7ad1d1042a8b28b7e5f3f70117c93b42716ffdd8ce86d808a" "q-49032/selection/acc83c74f03748b2e35d16f5b1d34df696d35c1cc9214487ece6a3d7d0147310"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-49032" :question/id #uuid "66a1c6ee-aed3-5592-86e6-e5621e001888" :question/math-academy-id "q-49032" :question/problem "For the function $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{2x+4}$ defined for $x\\in (-2,\\infty ),$ which of the following statements are true?" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{3{x}^{2}\\cdot (2x+4)-2({x}^{3})}{(2x+4)^{2}}=\\frac{4{x}^{3}+12{x}^{2}}{(2x+4)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ on $(-2,\\infty )$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4{x}^{3}+12{x}^{2}}{(2x+4)^{2}}=0 \\\\ 4{x}^{3}+12{x}^{2}=0 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}(x+3)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $x=0$ is the only stationary point of $f(x)$ on $(-2,\\infty ).$ Next, to determine whether this point is local maximum or minimum, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-1,1$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-1) & =\\frac{4(-1)^{3}+12(-1)^{2}}{(2(-1)+4)^{2}}=2>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{4(1)^{3}+12(1)^{2}}{(2(1)+4)^{2}}=\\frac{4}{9}>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,0)$ | $0$ | $(0,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-1$ |  | $1$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ |  | $↗$ |\n\nThe first derivative does not change sign at $x=0.$ Therefore, we conclude that $x=0$ is neither a minimum nor a maximum.\n\nHence, the first derivative test shows that the function has no local extrema." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-49032/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff7c597d-d4a4-5567-add6-eac77475b0d6"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-49032"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-49030"] :question/problem "Given the function $f(x)=-{x}^{5},$ which of the following statements are true?" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=-5{x}^{4}.\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n-5{x}^{4}=0\\,⟹\\,x=0.\n$$\n\nSo $x=0$ is the only stationary point of $f(x).$ Next, to determine whether this point is local maximum or minimum, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-1,1$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-1) & =-5(-1)^{4}=-5<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =-5(1)^{4}=-5<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,0)$ | $0$ | $(0,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-1$ |  | $1$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↘$ |  | $↘$ |\n\nThe first derivative does not change sign at $x=0.$ Therefore, we conclude that $x=0$ is neither a minimum nor a maximum.\n\nHence, the first derivative test shows that the function has no local extrema." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-3727"] :question/problem "Determine the local maxima and minima for $f(x) = 6x-x^3-5,$ and classify each point." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\nf'(x) = 6-3x^2.\n$$\n\nSolving $f'(x) = 0$ gives\n\n$$\n3(2-x^2)=0 \\quad \\Longrightarrow \\quad x=\\pm \\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nSo we have two stationary points, $x=\\pm \\sqrt 2.$ Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x = -2, 0, 2$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-2) & =6-3(-2)^{2}=-6 \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =6-3(0)^{2}=6 \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =6-3(2)^{2}=-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $\\left(-\\infty,-\\sqrt{2}\\right)$ | $-\\sqrt{2}$ | $\\left(-\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2}\\right)$ | $\\sqrt{2}$ | $\\left(\\sqrt{2},\\infty\\right)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-2$ |  | $0$ |  | $2$ |\n| $f'$ | $\\color{blue}-$ | $0$ | $\\color{red}+$ | $0$ | $\\color{blue}-$ |\n| $f$ | $\\color{blue}\\searrow$ | min | $\\color{red}\\nearrow$ | max | $\\color{blue}\\searrow$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical points.\n\n- At $x=-\\sqrt{2},$ the first derivative changes sign from negative to positive. Therefore, $x=-\\sqrt{2}$ is a local minimum.\n\n- At $x=\\sqrt{2},$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=\\sqrt{2}$ is a local maximum.\n\nWe conclude that $f(x)$ has one local minimum at $x = -\\sqrt 2$ and one local maximum at $x = \\sqrt 2.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-3730"] :question/problem "Find the relative maximum and minimum points of the function $f(x) = (2x+3)e^x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =(2x+3){e}^{x}+2{e}^{x}=(2x+5){e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving $f'(x)=0$  gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2x+5){e}^{x} & =0 \\\\ 2x+5 & =0 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $x = -\\dfrac{5}{2}$ is a stationary point. Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x = -5,0$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-5) & =(2(-5)+5){e}^{-5}=-\\frac{5}{{e}^{5}}<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =(2(0)+5){e}^{0}=5>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $\\left(-\\infty, -\\dfrac{5}{2}\\right)$ | $-\\dfrac{5}{2}$ | $\\left( -\\dfrac{5}{2},\\infty\\right)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-5$ |  | $0$ |\n| $f'$ | $\\color{blue}-$ | $0$ | $\\color{red}+$ |\n| $f$ | $\\color{blue}\\searrow$ | min | $\\color{red}\\nearrow$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical point.\n\nAt $x=-\\dfrac{5}{2},$ the first derivative changes sign from negative to positive.\n\nSo, we conclude that $f(x)$ has a local minimum at $x = -\\dfrac{5}{2}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-3729"] :question/worked-solution "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\nf'(x) \t=3x^2,\n$$\n\nand solving $f'(x) = 0$ gives\n\n$$\n3x^2 = 0 \\quad \\Longrightarrow \\quad x=0.\n$$\n\nSo $x = 0$ is the only stationary point of $f(x).$ Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x = -1,1$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-1) & =3(-1)^{2}=3>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =3(1)^{2}=3>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $\\left(-\\infty, 0\\right)$ | $0$ | $\\left(0,\\infty\\right)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-1$ |  | $1$ |\n| $f'$ | $\\color{red}+$ | $0$ | $\\color{red}+$ |\n| $f$ | $\\color{red}\\nearrow$ |  | $\\color{red}\\nearrow$ |\n\nThe first derivative does not change sign at $x=0.$ Therefore, we conclude that $x=0$ is neither a minimum nor a maximum.\n\nSo, the first derivative test shows that the function has no local extrema." :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
